usr_prompt:  Context: Health insurance insulates patients from the expense of medical care, giving doctors almost complete discretion in deciding the course of most medical treatments. Moreover, with doctors being paid for each procedure performed, they have an incentive to overtreat patients. It is thus clear that medical procedures administered by doctors are frequently prescribed only because these procedures lead to financial rewards.
Question: The argument uses which one of the following questionable techniques?
A. depicting choices as having been made arbitrarily by dismissing without argument reasons that have been given for these choices
B. presenting as capricious and idiosyncratic decisions that are based on the rigorous application of well-defined principles
C. inferring the performance of certain actions on no basis other than the existence of both incentive and opportunity for performing those actions
D. assuming that the irrelevance of a consideration for one participant in a decision makes that consideration irrelevant for each participant in the decision
